  "worked_solution": "**Method 1** \n\nThe distance between two points is the length of the line segment that connects them.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the length of the segment is $3$ units, so the distance equals $3.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/67c27f3a028ac71fdc9402fca5626a6a64cf9ddb9e643aa7cfab666c0d90ad5e.png)\n\n**Method 2**\n\nWe can find the distance between the two points by finding the distance of each point from the $x$-axis and then subtracting.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14146131/assets/d01cf6cffb0eacaa01cab5cc386ebe724d3a0647b8d923c787ebbbe3e50ee396.png)\n\nThe point $(6,-5)$ is at a distance of $5$ from the $x$-axis, and the point $(6,-2)$ is at a distance of $2$ from the $x$-axis. Therefore, the distance between the two points is $5-2=3.$",
